Fig. 2. Overview of the biosynthetic pathway of paclitaxel. The diterpene precursor GGPP is generated by the MEP pathway and the MVA pathway (highlighted in 
pink). GGPP undergoes cyclization via TXS to produce taxadiene. Taxadiene is then modified through a series of CYP450s and acyltransferases to yield the key 
intermediate baccatin III (highlighted in light yellow). The synthesis of the phenylisoserine side chain is highlighted in blue. Baccatin III is acetylated at the C13 
position with the phenylisoserine side chain, followed by hydroxylation and benzoylation at the C2' and C3' positions of the side chain, ultimately resulting in the 
production of paclitaxel (highlighted in light blue). OPP represents diphosphate. Enzyme abbreviations: TXS, taxadiene synthase; T5αH, taxadiene 5α-hydroxylase; 
TAT, taxadiene-5α-ol-O-acetyl transferase; T10βH, taxane-10β-hydroxylase; T13αH, taxane-13α-hydroxylase; T1βH, taxane 1β-hydroxylase; T9αH, taxane 9α-hy­
droxylase; T9αO, taxane 9α-oxidase; T2αH, taxane 2α-hydroxylase; T7βH, taxane 7β-hydroxylase; C4-C20, C4-C20 epoxidase; TBT, taxane-2α-O-benzoyl transferase; 
DBAT, 10-deacetylbaccatin III-10-O-acetyl transferase; PAM, phenylalanine ammonia-lyase; PCL, β-phenylalanine-CoA ligase; BAPT, 13-phenylpropanoyltransferase; 
DBTNBT, 3'-N-debenzoyl-2′-deoxytaxol N-benzoyltransferase.
